keyattestation — vendored copy of the Android Key Attestation Verifier
======================================================================

Upstream project: https://github.com/android/keyattestation
Pinned commit:    100dedaea2387d9705d29b2be3177466c33ae17b (2026-09-11)
Upstream license: Apache License 2.0 — full text in LICENSE (kept from
                  upstream, unmodified).

This directory contains a source-level copy ("vendoring") of the upstream
project, which is Google's official and actively maintained Android Key
Attestation certificate-chain verifier. Upstream does not publish to Maven;
embedding the sources is the intended usage pattern (see upstream README).

Modifications in this copy
--------------------------
1. Removed the JVM command-line tools and their tests:
     src/main/kotlin/VerifierCli.kt
     src/main/kotlin/CertPrinter.kt
     src/test/kotlin/VerifierCliTest.kt
     src/test/kotlin/CertPrinterTest.kt
2. Moved the `testing/` support package (cert factories, fakes) from the main
   source set to the test source set, so it does not ship in the app:
     src/main/kotlin/testing/ -> src/test/kotlin/testing/
3. Replaced the upstream build-time source generation for `GoogleTrustAnchors`
   (a Gradle task inlining roots.json as a Kotlin string constant) with a
   hand-written runtime loader that reads the same roots.json from the
   classpath: src/main/kotlin/GoogleTrustAnchors.kt.
   The roots.json data file itself is vendored unmodified at the module root;
   it is copied into the runtime classpath by `processResources`.
4. Build script rewritten for the host project (build.gradle.kts):
     - Kotlin 1.9.24 (upstream: 2.2.0), JVM target 17 (upstream: Java 21);
     - dependency versions pinned to releases consumable by Kotlin 1.9;
     - guava switched to the `-android` variant (upstream: `-jre`);
     - dependency scopes adjusted to `api` where upstream public signatures
       reference guava / protobuf types;
     - the upstream `test-logger` Gradle plugin is not used.

All vendored Kotlin sources and data files other than the changes above are
byte-identical to the pinned upstream revision; this was verified by hash
comparison when the copy was imported.

